t1 Precompilation

p There are 3 precompilation steps in ZINC: P1 to P3. The main objective is to \
provide a more data structured ZCS for compilation instead of raw text files.

p The precompilation process is run on the very first file given to compile. \
However, its execution is not completely straightforward. These 3 steps will \
be executed as much as required, depending on dependencies involved in the \
program, making it a recursive process. More details in step P3.

p At start, we parse the Z file given as input, character per character from \
beginning to end.




b
t2 P1 - Comments and Text

t3 Objective

p The 1st step of the precompiler concerns comments removal and text \
transformation. The objective is to remove every kind of literal text that can \
perturbate further parsing.

p This must be done as the very beginning of everything, because text and \
comments can contain any kind of information, including complete parts of Z \
code, precompiler directives or anything.

p Also, these 2 operations must be done at the same time because a string can \
contain a comment indicator or vice versa... We must know if we are in a \
comment or in a string to interpret correctly the character we are currently \
analyzing.


t3 Comments

p Nothing really important to say here, just complete removal of everything \
between single line and multi-line comments. Everything contained in a comment \
is ignored until its end, even if another sub-comment is involved, an escape \
character (\m\\\m) is used or anything.

p We can't escape a comment. It is the most powerful syntax tool in the \
language as it allows the compiler to see or not some part of the original file.


t3 Text

p Here, the word "text" refers to both characters and strings as in the Z \
programming language define them, \uunder their literal representation\u \
(using simple quotes \m'\m delimiters for characters and double quotes \
delimiter \m"\m for strings).

p String content is stored as multi-byte sequence to remove any potential \
syntaxical character from code. To be interpreted as a real string at the end, \
it is wrapped under the following:
" z Figure 1
//original code
a = "hello"

//after p1 step
a = str{
    kind = ^Str.KIND.LIT,
    dat  = ^Str.lit {
        len=...,  //length is also computed
        dat=``... //multi-byte sequence
    }
}
"
p Characters are also replaced by their corresponding byte sequence. Note that \
literal characters must contain only one byte. Non-ASCII characters can be \
used, but multi-byte characters will produce an error.

p Also, escaping character \m\\\m is taken into account in text parsing so \
that special characters can be ignored (\m'\m or \m"\m for example), and some \
meta characters can be invoqued (\m\\n\m or \m\\a\m for example). If an \
escaped character is not special or does not correspond to a meta character, \
it raises an error.




t2 P2 - Directives

t3 Objective

p This step will transform precompiler directives into Z code. Z precompiler \
directives are made to manipulate text fragments more easilly among the Z code \
itself. After this step, no more precompiler directive should remain. If so, \
error(s) will be raised.

p Precompiler directives \uwill \bNOT\b interact the real Z code\u, they only \
manipulate \utextual fragments\u, as an upper layer, in addition to the real \
code.

p As for previous steps, parsing is made by reading the whole file byte per \
byte, but this time, we may need to operate multiple readings. Infact, when a \
directive is processed, the original text is replaced in the file (in process \
memory, no changes in the actual file), and these changes can involve other \
directives in the new content.


t3 Parsing process

p The process is made as a loop: First, we read the whole file, looking for a \
\m#\m character and process the directive (if possible). If it does not \
correspond to a directive pattern, it is simply skipped. Else, we try to \
process the directive according to its kind.

p Each directive has its own requirements to be executed properly. If \
everything is okay, we can emit a replacement text for that directive as a \
result. Going back to our global parsing, we can now replace the directive \
text with this.

p It is important to keep the reading head \b\ubefore\u\b directive text when \
parsing it because the replacement text \bmust also be parsed\b. If the \
directive couldn't be processed, a "PCPL failure item" is created as internal \
object, and parsing continues skipping this \m#\m character.

p A PCPL failure doesn't mean there is an error somewhere in the given text. \
It rather means the directive couldn't be processed \b\uyet\u\b for some \
reason. Having a directive inside another one can be the reason. Infact, in \
that case, the inner directive must be processed first to allow the outter one \
to have a full valid content.

p Depending on if a replacement has been made during parsing or not, when we \
finished parsing the whole file content, we will go over it one more time, or \
stop here. "No more changes" means "we have been through all the text at least \
once." so no need to parse again.

p This re-parsing is also limited to avoid infinite recursivity in directive \
definitions. This is the role of the compilation option \mPCPL_DIRECTIVES_MAX_RETRIES\m.


t3 Additional details

p Precompiler arithmetic directive allow the use of the following operators: \
\m-\m, \m+\m, \m*\m, \m/\m, \m&\m, \m|\m and \m^\m. These are purely \
\unumerical\u operations (no text or collection-like behavior). Operands are \
being read as decimal numbers within 64b range (from 0 to 2**64-1 included).

p Arithmetic operations are split in ranks to allow a bit of priorization: \
Higher priority operators are computed first, with their side operands, and \
then, we proceed to lower ranks. There are 2 ranks right now: \
l 1st rank (highest priority): \m*\m, \m/\m, \m&\m, \m|\m, \m^\m
l 2nd rank (lowest  priority): \m-\m, \m+\m

p \uNote:\u At the end of that step, every \m#\m remaining in the code should \
refer to an FSZ operator.




t2 P3 - Segregate ZCIs

t3 Objective

p This step is the first to really consider the Z code syntax itself. The \
objective is to split our piece of code (still raw text until now) into a \
valid ZCS.

p In that step, we won't analyze ZCIs themselves but their delimitations. \
We simply read the code as we did before, but this time, we are storing it \
under text fragments, in ZCIs objects.

p These objects keeps the information of their context (where do they come \
from: filename, line, column...), and some other useful information such as \
the indexes of every includer detected inside and their corresponding peer. \
These informations are crucial as we will only deal with ZCI objects during \
the compilation process.

p Of course, to determine the beginning and end of a ZCI, the rules described \
in the ZPL documentation are applied (semicolon, escape character for line \
feeds, includers).


t3 Imports

p An other thing that is done during that step is the decomposition of EXT_IMP \
ZCIs. Each time we are going to store a ZCI, we look if it is an EXT_IMP. If \
so, instead of storing it, we will try to import the file and run the full \
precompiler on it (3 steps).

p Processing an import is a recursive operation. As soon as it is done, the \
resulted ZCIs are stored instead of the EXT_IMP originally detected. In any \
other case, we simply store the ZCI as is (to be parsed in further steps).

p Because of the recursivity behavior of importations, each time we go into \
it, the current file and context we were in are saved somewhere else, and we \
go into a "sub-context".

p Of course, if a sub-context for a certain file has already been opened for a \
file, it cannot be opened again. However, for a better user experience, the \
ZPL allows you to import a file several times.

p To respect that, if an importation for a file has alread been made, it will \
simply be ignored next time it will be imported. This way, you can possibly \
forget to import some resource, but you will not struggle with libraries \
requirering dependencies at multiple levels.

b
p After this, every sub-context is closed, and the compilation process can \
really start, using only the given list of unparsed ZCIs as input. (You may \
have understood that no EXT_IMP ZCI remains among them)



b
